  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 142 PM MDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nIt has become unstable over the Palmer Divide and eastern plains \nwith SBCAPE of 400-1000 J/kg. This combined with good shear is\nexpected to produce a few strong to severe thunderstorms. Main \narea of concern is from the Palmer Divide northeast to Akron to\nSterling and eastward. Farther west, subsidence is expected to \nlimit convective activity this afternoon, but this may change this\nevening.\u00a0Models have been trending towards scattered to numerous \nthunderstorms developing over southeast Colorado tonight, after \nsunset. This activity appears to be triggered by the left exit \nregion of a jet streak moving through. The uncertainty in the \nforecast is how far north the thunderstorms will develop. The 12Z \nNAMNest and some of the HRRR runs show storms as far north as the \nDenver metro area in the 02-04Z time frame. Hail will be the main \nthreat given the steep lapse rates and cooler mid level \ntemperatures. The thunderstorms are expected to continue after \nmidnight over southeast Colorado and then exit the state.\n\nWeak riding\u00a0builds over Colorado Monday bringing dry conditions. \nTemperatures will be a little\u00a0cooler than what we have \nrecently\u00a0seen, but they will be near normal with highs in the mid \n70s across northeast Colorado. Models show light \nprecipitation\u00a0forming over the eastern plains (south of I-76) \nMonday night. Confidence isn't the highest that we will see rain \nshowers or thunderstorms Monday night, but we have enough moisture\nand instability for them. If we get enough\u00a0lift, some showers or \nstorms could develop. The models do show an increasing jet over \nthe area. Therefore, we will include some PoPs in the forecast for\nthis. \u00a0\u00a0 A closed low will be over southern Nevada on Monday. It \nweakens and lifts northeast across Utah and Wyoming Tuesday and \nTuesday night in the southwest flow aloft. Best lift with this \nfeature is on track to be later on Tuesday with Tuesday evening \nseeing the best chance for showers/storms. Temperatures warm with \nhighs in the mid 70s to lower 80s.\u00a0\n\nOn Wednesday, temperatures will be a few degrees warmer with \nhighs in the upper 70s to lower 80s. Chances for showers and \nthunderstorms look low at this time, but they can't be ruled out \nwith slightly above-normal moisture lingering around.\u00a0\n\nFor Thursday through Sunday, an upper level high will be over \nTexas and northern Mexico with an upper level trough roughly \ncentered over the Pacific Northwest. This will maintain\u00a0a \nsouthwest flow aloft over the Central Rockies. Models hint at some\nsort of shortwave trough lifting northward out of the subtropics \nfor Thursday. The timing and location of the trough and its \nassociated precipitation are not agreed upon by the models. \nHowever, models are starting to show it will be moist with \nprecipitable water values climbing well above normal reaching \n130-180 percent of normal and slightly higher on the GFS. \nPrecipitable water values will stay near or slightly above normal \nFriday through the weekend. Main chance for showers/storms will be\nover the mountains, but we could see them spread onto the plains \nif we get a disturbance or two to help with lift.\u00a0Temperatures \nduring this period are expected to be slightly above normal, with \nhighs in the upper 70s to lower 80s. Temperatures will be closer \nto normal on the days with more moisture/cloud cover.",
